WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.216 --> 00:00:16.200
It was late morning when Oliver awoke from a long, sound sleep.

2
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.121
There was no one else in the room except Fagin, who was boiling some coffee in a pot

3
00:00:21.141 --> 00:00:21.761
for breakfast.

4
00:00:22.741 --> 00:00:27.123
The old man was whistling to himself as he stirred the coffee round and round with an iron

5
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:32.184
spoon. Fagin stopped to listen whenever he heard the smallest sound downstairs.

6
00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:37.018
But once he was satisfied no one was there, he went back to stirring and whistling.

7
00:00:38.039 --> 00:00:41.861
Oliver watched Fagin through half-closed eyes as he drifted in and out of sleep.

8
00:00:42.881 --> 00:00:45.162
When the coffee was done, Fagin turned around.

9
00:00:46.323 --> 00:00:47.924
"Oliver!" he called softly.

10
00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:51.386
Oliver didn't respond, pretending to be asleep.

11
00:00:52.246 --> 00:00:54.287
Fagin stepped gently to the door and latched it.

12
00:00:55.128 --> 00:00:57.969
Then he pulled a small box from a trapdoor in the floor.

13
00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.432
Dragging an old chair to the table, he sat down and opened the box.

14
00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:09.476
Oliver's eyes widened as Fagin pulled out a magnificent gold watch, sparkling with jewels.

15
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:13.739
The old man grinned and muttered some words that Oliver couldn't hear.

16
00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:19.042
Out of the box came a dozen more watches, plus rings, necklaces, and bracelets.

17
00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:22.845
Suddenly Fagin looked up, and his eyes met Oliver's.

18
00:01:23.846 --> 00:01:26.267
Fagin closed the lid of the box with a loud crash.

19
00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:29.149
Trembling with anger, he approached Oliver.

20
00:01:29.169 --> 00:01:31.331
"Why are you watching me?

21
00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:33.032
Why are you awake?

22
00:01:33.972 --> 00:01:34.913
What have you seen?"

23
00:01:35.293 --> 00:01:35.933
cried Fagin.

24
00:01:36.954 --> 00:01:37.654
"Speak, boy!"

25
00:01:37.674 --> 00:01:41.456
"I couldn't sleep any longer, sir," Oliver said meekly.

26
00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:44.478
"I'm very sorry if I disturbed you, sir."

27
00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:47.900
"Did you see any of these pretty things, my dear?"

28
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.181
Fagin laid his hand on the box.

29
00:01:50.201 --> 00:01:51.882
"Yes, sir."

30
00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:55.324
"Ah!" Fagin turned pale.

31
00:01:55.344 --> 00:01:58.325
"They they're mine, Oliver.

32
00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:00.446
My little property.

33
00:02:01.307 --> 00:02:03.288
All I have to live on in my old age.

34
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.961
People call me a miser, my dear.

35
00:02:06.862 --> 00:02:08.363
I'm just a miser, that's all."

36
00:02:09.363 --> 00:02:11.645
Oliver thought the old gentleman must be a miser.

37
00:02:12.585 --> 00:02:15.947
Why else would he live in such a dirty place when he had all those riches?

38
00:02:16.988 --> 00:02:19.549
But maybe he was spending all his money caring for the boys.

39
00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:21.390
"May I get up?"

40
00:02:21.791 --> 00:02:22.391
asked Oliver.

41
00:02:22.411 --> 00:02:25.033
"Certainly, my dear, certainly.

42
00:02:25.873 --> 00:02:27.394
There's a pitcher of water by the door.

43
00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:30.376
Bring it here and I'll give you a basin to wash in."

44
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.263
After Oliver washed himself, Fagin told him to empty the basin out the window.

45
00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:40.948
Oliver had just finished doing that when the Dodger returned with one of the boys, Charley Bates.

46
00:02:42.049 --> 00:02:43.330
They all sat down to breakfast.

47
00:02:44.111 --> 00:02:47.153
The Dodger had brought home some rolls and ham in the crown of his hat.

48
00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:51.697
Fagin glanced slyly at Oliver as he spoke to the boys.

49
00:02:52.718 --> 00:02:54.940
"Have you been hard at work this morning, my dears?"

50
00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.942
"Oh yes," said Charley.

51
00:02:57.962 --> 00:02:59.604
"Good boys!

52
00:03:00.488 --> 00:03:01.549
What have you got, Dodger?"

53
00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:03.469
"A couple of wallets."

54
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.390
The Dodger handed two wallets to Fagin.

55
00:03:07.251 --> 00:03:09.752
Fagin opened the wallets and inspected the insides.

56
00:03:09.772 --> 00:03:13.793
"Nicely lined, but not as heavy as they might be.

57
00:03:14.674 --> 00:03:16.635
But very neat and nicely made.

58
00:03:17.595 --> 00:03:20.416
The Dodger is an ingenious workman, isn't he, Oliver?"

59
00:03:20.436 --> 00:03:23.217
"Indeed, sir," replied Oliver.

60
00:03:24.298 --> 00:03:29.480
Charley laughed uproariously at this remark to the amazement of Oliver, who didn't see anything to laugh at.

61
00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:31.893
"And what have you got, my dear?"

62
00:03:32.213 --> 00:03:33.113
Fagin asked Charley.

63
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:38.856
"Handkerchiefs." Charley pulled four silk squares from his pockets.

64
00:03:39.936 --> 00:03:41.337
Fagin looked them over carefully.

65
00:03:41.357 --> 00:03:43.457
"Well, they're very good.

66
00:03:44.298 --> 00:03:46.339
But you haven't monogrammed them nicely at all.

67
00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:50.220
All the embroidered initials will have to be picked out with a needle.

68
00:03:51.161 --> 00:03:52.861
We'll teach Oliver how to do that.

69
00:03:53.822 --> 00:03:56.943
You'd like to make handkerchiefs as easily as Charley, wouldn't you?"

70
00:03:56.963 --> 00:03:58.804
"Very much indeed.

71
00:03:59.752 --> 00:04:01.694
If you'll teach me, sir," replied Oliver.

72
00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.717
Charley laughed so hard that he almost choked on his coffee.

73
00:04:05.737 --> 00:04:09.921
"He's so innocent," Charley said when he could speak again.

74
00:04:10.962 --> 00:04:14.124
Oliver didn't realize that he'd fallen in with a gang of pickpockets.

75
00:04:15.305 --> 00:04:16.847
Sometimes the boys stole wallets.

76
00:04:17.928 --> 00:04:21.411
And sometimes they took handkerchiefs, which were later resold in shops.

77
00:04:22.472 --> 00:04:26.955
When breakfast was cleared away, Fagin and the two boys played a game that seemed very strange to

78
00:04:27.076 --> 00:04:33.463
Oliver. First Fagin placed a snuffbox in one pocket of his trousers and a wallet in another.

79
00:04:34.464 --> 00:04:37.946
He put a watch in his waistcoat pocket and a mock diamond pin in his shirt.

80
00:04:38.966 --> 00:04:43.169
He buttoned his coat and put his eyeglass case and his handkerchief in his coat pockets.

81
00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:48.913
Then he trotted back and forth with a walking stick, imitating the way that old gentlemen walked about

82
00:04:48.953 --> 00:04:49.953
the city every day.

83
00:04:51.014 --> 00:04:53.596
Sometimes he stopped, pretending to study a shop window.

84
00:04:54.612 --> 00:04:59.296
He patted his pockets and looked around in such a funny way that Oliver laughed until he cried.

85
00:05:00.337 --> 00:05:05.541
At last the Dodger stepped on Fagin's toes while Charley stumbled into him and reached into his pockets.

86
00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:10.625
If Fagin felt a hand in any pocket, he cried out and the game began again.

87
00:05:11.586 --> 00:05:15.449
But the game was soon interrupted by the arrival of a young lady named Nancy.

88
00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:19.212
"Pleased to meet you, Oliver," said Nancy.

89
00:05:20.233 --> 00:05:24.196
Oliver thought she seemed very nice, almost like an older sister to the boys.

90
00:05:25.288 --> 00:05:29.809
Fagin gave her something to eat, and after chatting awhile, she left with Charley and the Dodger.

91
00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:32.590
"This is a pleasant life, isn't it?"

92
00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:33.870
Fagin said to Oliver.

93
00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.031
"They've gone out for the day."

94
00:05:36.051 --> 00:05:38.392
"Are they going to do some more work?"

95
00:05:39.432 --> 00:05:41.752
"Yes, if there's work to do, they'll find it.

96
00:05:42.813 --> 00:05:44.573
Make them your models, my dear."

97
00:05:45.593 --> 00:05:49.174
Fagin tapped the fire shovel on the hearth to add emphasis to his words.

98
00:05:49.194 --> 00:05:54.616
"Do everything they say and take all their advice, especially the Dodger's.

99
00:05:55.636 --> 00:05:59.998
He'll be a great man and he'll make you one, too, if you model yourself after him."

100
00:06:00.999 --> 00:06:02.500
Fagin stared off into the fire.

101
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.202
"Is my handkerchief hanging out of my pocket, my dear?"

102
00:06:06.222 --> 00:06:08.463
"Yes, sir."

103
00:06:08.483 --> 00:06:12.105
"See if you can take it out without my feeling anything.

104
00:06:13.046 --> 00:06:15.807
Remember how you saw them do that when they were playing earlier."

105
00:06:16.848 --> 00:06:20.710
Oliver held up the bottom of Fagin's pocket with one hand as he had seen the Dodger hold

106
00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.512
it. Then with the other hand, he pulled out the handkerchief.

107
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.320
"Is it gone?"

108
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:27.341
cried Fagin.

109
00:06:27.361 --> 00:06:29.121
"Here it is, sir."

110
00:06:30.021 --> 00:06:31.342
Oliver showed him the handkerchief.

111
00:06:31.362 --> 00:06:34.262
"I never saw a sharper lad!

112
00:06:35.183 --> 00:06:36.263
Here's a shilling for you.

113
00:06:37.203 --> 00:06:39.624
If you go on this way, you'll be the greatest man of all.

114
00:06:40.604 --> 00:06:43.805
Now come here and I'll show you how to take the monograms out of handkerchiefs."

115
00:06:45.005 --> 00:06:47.926
Oliver didn't understand what this had to do with being a great man.

116
00:06:48.906 --> 00:06:53.367
But assuming that the older man knew more than he did, Oliver followed him to the table and

117
00:06:53.407 --> 00:06:55.308
was soon engrossed in his new work.
